# Line endings, decided once.
#
# Until 2026-08-14 this repo had both: 584 of 2864 tracked files were CRLF in their committed blobs
# and the rest LF, because different tools wrote them. Nothing read them worse for it, but every
# tool that rewrites a file whole — Python's write_text on Windows translates every newline —
# turned a one-line edit into a whole-file diff. One 71-line change was committed as 2707 changed
# lines, and a 394-line change as 31,678. A diff like that cannot be reviewed, and a repo where the
# answer depends on which tool touched the file last has no answer at all.
#
# Everything text is LF in the repository. Windows working copies get LF too, which every editor
# and every tool in this stack reads; the two exceptions that genuinely need CRLF are declared below.

* text=auto eol=lf

# The app-catalog page is built (esbuild) from the modular sources under
# aimeat/src/static/app-catalog/ into a single artifact. These two lines pre-date the rule above and
# are kept explicit: `pnpm check:app-catalog` compares the built artifact byte for byte, so its
# inputs and output must round-trip identically whatever a contributor's core.autocrlf says. The
# blanket rule now covers them, and naming them again costs nothing and survives a change to it.
aimeat/src/static/app-catalog.html      text eol=lf
aimeat/src/static/app-catalog/**        text eol=lf

# Windows shells parse these by line and want CRLF on disk.
*.bat  text eol=crlf
*.cmd  text eol=crlf

# Binary, declared rather than detected: git's heuristic is good and this is not a place to rely on
# a heuristic, because a false "text" here corrupts the file on checkout.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.webp binary
*.mp3  binary
*.mp4  binary
*.wav  binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf  binary
*.otf  binary
*.eot  binary
*.pdf  binary
*.zip  binary
*.gz   binary
*.wasm binary
*.db   binary
*.sqlite binary
*.sqlite3 binary
